<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-functions">
<title>Functions</title>

<introduction>
<remark>
<title>Prerequisites</title>
<p>Familiarity with sets from <xref ref="sec-elementary-set-theory" /> and relations
from <xref ref="sec-relations" />.</p>
</remark>

<remark>
<title>Learning Objectives</title>
<p>By the end of this section, the reader should be able to:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>define a function as a special type of relation;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish domain, codomain, and range;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>interpret function notation and mapping notation;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>evaluate functions and determine equality of functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize identity, constant, inclusion, and piecewise-defined functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>determine whether a function is injective, surjective, or bijective;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compose functions and interpret the order of composition;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand inverse functions and the connection between invertibility
          and bijectivity;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>work with images and preimages of sets;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>interpret sequences and multivariable functions as functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize common errors involving functions.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</remark>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-why-functions-matter">
<title>Why Functions Matter</title>
<p>Functions are among the most central objects in mathematics.</p>

<p>They describe how one quantity, object, or structure depends on another.</p>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>the area of a circle as a function of its radius;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>position as a function of time;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>temperature as a function of location;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>probability as a function of an event;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>a matrix transformation acting on vectors;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>a derivative assigning a new function to a differentiable function;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>a statistical model mapping data to predictions.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Nearly every branch of mathematics uses functions in some form.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-functions-special-relations">
<title>Functions as Special Relations</title>
<p>Recall that a relation from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m> is a subset of</p>

<p>
<md>A\times B.</md>
</p>

<p>A function is a relation with an additional requirement.</p>

<definition>
<title>Function</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>function</em> <m>f</m> from a set <m>A</m> to a set <m>B</m> is a relation that
associates each element of <m>A</m> with exactly one element of <m>B</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Thus every input must have one and only one output.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-function-mapping-notation">
<title>Function Mapping Notation</title>
<p>A function from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m> is commonly written</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B.</md>
</p>

<p>The arrow</p>

<p>
<md>\to</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>maps to</q></p>

<p>in this context.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ maps @@PROTECTED1@@ to @@PROTECTED2@@</q></p>

<p>or</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ is a function from @@PROTECTED1@@ to @@PROTECTED2@@.</q></p>

<p>The same arrow has different readings in other contexts. For example,
<m>x\to a</m> in calculus is read <q>@@PROTECTED1@@ approaches @@PROTECTED2@@.</q> Context determines
the intended meaning.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-mapsto-symbol">
<title>The Mapsto Symbol</title>
<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\mapsto</md>
</p>

<p>is called the <em>mapsto symbol</em> and is read</p>

<p><q>maps to.</q></p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>x\mapsto x^2</md>
</p>

<p>means that the input <m>x</m> is assigned the output <m>x^2</m>.</p>

<p>A complete description may be written</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R\to\R,\qquad x\mapsto x^2.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-function-notation">
<title>Function Notation</title>
<p>If <m>f</m> is a function and <m>x</m> is an input, the corresponding output is
written</p>

<p>
<md>f(x).</md>
</p>

<p>This is read</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ of @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<p>The parentheses do not mean multiplication.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)</md>
</p>

<p>does not mean</p>

<p>
<md>f\cdot x.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-function-domain">
<title>Domain</title>
<definition>
<title>Domain</title>
<statement>
<p>For a function</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B,</md>
</p>

<p>the set <m>A</m> is called the <em>domain</em> of <m>f</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The domain is the set of allowed inputs.</p>

<p>It may be written</p>

<p>
<md>\dom(f)=A.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-function-codomain">
<title>Codomain</title>
<definition>
<title>Codomain</title>
<statement>
<p>For a function</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B,</md>
</p>

<p>the set <m>B</m> is called the <em>codomain</em> of <m>f</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The codomain is the set in which the outputs are required to lie.</p>

<p>It may contain elements that are never actually produced by the function.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-function-range">
<title>Range or Image</title>
<definition>
<title>Range</title>
<statement>
<p>The <em>range</em>, or <em>image</em>, of a function <m>f:A\to B</m> is the set of
actual outputs:</p>

<p>
<md>\Image(f)=\{f(x):x\in A\}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The notation</p>

<p>
<md>\Image(f)</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>the image of @@PROTECTED0@@.</q></p>

<p>The range always satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>\Image(f)\subseteq B.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-domain-codomain-range">
<title>Domain, Codomain, and Range</title>
<p>These three concepts should not be confused.</p>

<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B,</md>
</p>

<p>we have:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><m>A</m>: domain;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>B</m>: codomain;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\Image(f)</m>: actual outputs.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>In general,</p>

<p>
<md>\Image(f)\subseteq B,</md>
</p>

<p>and equality need not hold.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-domain-codomain-range-example">
<title>Example of Domain, Codomain, and Range</title>
<p>Define</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R\to\R,\qquad f(x)=x^2.</md>
</p>

<p>The domain is</p>

<p>
<md>\R.</md>
</p>

<p>The codomain is also</p>

<p>
<md>\R.</md>
</p>

<p>But the range is</p>

<p>
<md>[0,\infty).</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\Image(f)\neq\R.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-evaluating-functions">
<title>Evaluating Functions</title>
<p>To evaluate a function, replace its input variable by the specified value.</p>

<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=3x^2-2x+1.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>f(2)=3(2)^2-2(2)+1=12-4+1=9.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-function-evaluation">
<title>Worked Example: Function Evaluation</title>
<example>
<title>Evaluate a Function</title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=2x^2-5x+3.</md>
</p>

<p>Find <m>f(-2)</m>.</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>f(-2)=21</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-functions-not-only-formulas">
<title>Functions Need Not Be Given by Formulas</title>
<p>A function may be described by:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>a formula;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>a table;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>a graph;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>an arrow diagram;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>an algorithm;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>a verbal rule;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>a set of ordered pairs.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>The essential requirement is not the presence of a formula.</p>

<p>The essential requirement is that each input have exactly one output.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-function-ordered-pairs">
<title>Functions as Sets of Ordered Pairs</title>
<p>A function may be represented as a set of ordered pairs.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>f=\{(1,a),(2,b),(3,b)\}</md>
</p>

<p>defines a function from</p>

<p>
<md>\{1,2,3\}</md>
</p>

<p>to a codomain containing <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.</p>

<p>The first coordinates are inputs and the second coordinates are outputs.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-not-a-function">
<title>When a Relation Is Not a Function</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p>
<md>R=\{(1,a),(1,b),(2,c)\}.</md>
</p>

<p>The input <m>1</m> is associated with two distinct outputs:</p>

<p>
<md>a\qquad\text{and}\qquad b.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore <m>R</m> is not a function.</p>

<p>By contrast,</p>

<p>
<md>\{(1,a),(2,a),(3,a)\}</md>
</p>

<p>is a function because several inputs may share the same output.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-exactly-one-output">
<title>Exactly One Output</title>
<p>The phrase <q>exactly one</q> combines two requirements:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>every input has at least one output;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>no input has more than one output.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>Both conditions are necessary.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-function-equality">
<title>Equality of Functions</title>
<p>Two functions are equal only when they have the same domain, the same
codomain, and assign the same output to every input.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>f=g</md>
</p>

<p>means that the functions agree as complete mappings, not merely that their
formulas look similar.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R\to\R,\qquad f(x)=x^2</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>g:[0,\infty)\to\R,\qquad g(x)=x^2</md>
</p>

<p>are not the same function because their domains differ.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-identity-functions">
<title>Identity Functions</title>
<definition>
<title>Identity Function</title>
<statement>
<p>For a set <m>A</m>, the <em>identity function</em> on <m>A</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{id}_A:A\to A,\qquad \operatorname{id}_A(x)=x.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The notation</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{id}_A</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>the identity on @@PROTECTED0@@.</q></p>

<p>The identity function leaves every element unchanged.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-constant-functions">
<title>Constant Functions</title>
<definition>
<title>Constant Function</title>
<statement>
<p>A function <m>f:A\to B</m> is <em>constant</em> if there exists a fixed
<m>c\in B</m> such that</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=c</md>
</p>

<p>for every <m>x\in A</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=5</md>
</p>

<p>assigns the same output to every input.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-inclusion-functions">
<title>Inclusion Functions</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>A\subseteq B,</md>
</p>

<p>the inclusion function is</p>

<p>
<md>\iota:A\to B,\qquad \iota(a)=a.</md>
</p>

<p>The Greek letter</p>

<p>
<md>\iota</md>
</p>

<p>is called <em>iota</em>, pronounced <q>eye-OH-tuh.</q></p>

<p>The inclusion function sends each element of the smaller set to the same
element viewed inside the larger set.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-piecewise-functions">
<title>Piecewise-Defined Functions</title>
<p>A function may use different formulas on different parts of its domain.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=
\begin{cases}
x^2,&amp;x\geq0,\\
-x,&amp;x&lt;0.
\end{cases}</md>
</p>

<p>This is called a <em>piecewise-defined function</em>.</p>

<p>The cases environment is read according to the conditions:</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ if @@PROTECTED1@@, and @@PROTECTED2@@ if @@PROTECTED3@@.</q></p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-evaluating-piecewise">
<title>Evaluating Piecewise Functions</title>
<p>For the function</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=
\begin{cases}
x^2,&amp;x\geq0,\\
-x,&amp;x&lt;0,
\end{cases}</md>
</p>

<p>we have</p>

<p>
<md>f(3)=3^2=9</md>
</p>

<p>because <m>3\geq0</m>.</p>

<p>But</p>

<p>
<md>f(-3)=-(-3)=3</md>
</p>

<p>because <m>-3&lt;0</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-restricted-domains">
<title>Restricted Domains</title>
<p>Sometimes a formula becomes useful as a function only after its domain is
restricted.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=\frac1x</md>
</p>

<p>cannot have <m>0</m> in its domain.</p>

<p>A natural real domain is therefore</p>

<p>
<md>\R\setminus\{0\}.</md>
</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=\sqrt x</md>
</p>

<p>has real-valued outputs only when</p>

<p>
<md>x\geq0.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus a natural real domain is</p>

<p>
<md>[0,\infty).</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-natural-domains">
<title>Natural Domains</title>
<p>When a formula is given without an explicit domain, the <em>natural domain</em>
is often taken to be the largest standard set of inputs for which the formula
is meaningful in the chosen number system.</p>

<p>For real-valued functions, examples include:</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=\frac1{x-2}</md>
</p>

<p>with natural domain</p>

<p>
<md>\R\setminus\{2\},</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>g(x)=\sqrt{x+4}</md>
</p>

<p>with natural domain</p>

<p>
<md>[-4,\infty).</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-injective-functions">
<title>Injective Functions</title>
<definition>
<title>Injective Function</title>
<statement>
<p>A function <m>f:A\to B</m> is <em>injective</em>, or <em>one-to-one</em>, if</p>

<p>
<md>f(x_1)=f(x_2)\Longrightarrow x_1=x_2.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>This means distinct inputs cannot produce the same output.</p>

<p>An equivalent statement is</p>

<p>
<md>x_1\neq x_2\Longrightarrow f(x_1)\neq f(x_2).</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-injective-example">
<title>Example of an Injective Function</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R\to\R,\qquad f(x)=2x+3.</md>
</p>

<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>f(x_1)=f(x_2).</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>2x_1+3=2x_2+3.</md>
</p>

<p>Subtracting <m>3</m> gives</p>

<p>
<md>2x_1=2x_2.</md>
</p>

<p>Dividing by <m>2</m> gives</p>

<p>
<md>x_1=x_2.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore <m>f</m> is injective.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-not-injective-example">
<title>A Function That Is Not Injective</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R\to\R,\qquad f(x)=x^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>f(2)=4</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>f(-2)=4,</md>
</p>

<p>distinct inputs produce the same output.</p>

<p>Therefore <m>f</m> is not injective.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-surjective-functions">
<title>Surjective Functions</title>
<definition>
<title>Surjective Function</title>
<statement>
<p>A function <m>f:A\to B</m> is <em>surjective</em>, or <em>onto</em>, if every
element of the codomain is the output of at least one input.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Symbolically,</p>

<p>
<md>\forall y\in B,\;\exists x\in A\text{ such that }f(x)=y.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\Image(f)=B.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-surjective-example">
<title>Example of a Surjective Function</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R\to\R,\qquad f(x)=x^3.</md>
</p>

<p>Given any</p>

<p>
<md>y\in\R,</md>
</p>

<p>choose</p>

<p>
<md>x=\sqrt[3]{y}.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=\left(\sqrt[3]{y}\right)^3=y.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore every real number has a preimage, and <m>f</m> is surjective.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-codomain-surjectivity">
<title>Codomain Matters for Surjectivity</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R\to\R,\qquad f(x)=x^2.</md>
</p>

<p>This function is not surjective because negative real numbers are never
outputs.</p>

<p>However,</p>

<p>
<md>g:\R\to[0,\infty),\qquad g(x)=x^2</md>
</p>

<p>is surjective.</p>

<p>Thus surjectivity depends on the specified codomain.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-bijective-functions">
<title>Bijective Functions</title>
<definition>
<title>Bijective Function</title>
<statement>
<p>A function is <em>bijective</em>, or a <em>bijection</em>, if it is both
injective and surjective.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>A bijection pairs the elements of the domain and codomain without omissions
or repetitions.</p>

<p>Each output has exactly one corresponding input.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-bijectivity">
<title>Worked Example: Testing Bijectivity</title>
<example>
<title>Determine Whether a Function Is Bijective</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R\to\R,\qquad f(x)=3x-5.</md>
</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p><m>f</m> is bijective.</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-function-composition">
<title>Composition of Functions</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>g:B\to C.</md>
</p>

<p>Their composition is</p>

<p>
<md>g\circ f:A\to C.</md>
</p>

<p>The expression</p>

<p>
<md>g\circ f</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ composed with @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<p>The definition is</p>

<p>
<md>(g\circ f)(x)=g(f(x)).</md>
</p>

<p>The function <m>f</m> acts first, then <m>g</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-composition-order">
<title>Order Matters in Composition</title>
<p>In general,</p>

<p>
<md>g\circ f\neq f\circ g.</md>
</p>

<p>For example, let</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=x+1,\qquad g(x)=x^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>(g\circ f)(x)=(x+1)^2,</md>
</p>

<p>while</p>

<p>
<md>(f\circ g)(x)=x^2+1.</md>
</p>

<p>These are different functions.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-composition-associativity">
<title>Associativity of Composition</title>
<p>Function composition is associative.</p>

<p>For compatible functions,</p>

<p>
<md>h\circ(g\circ f)=(h\circ g)\circ f.</md>
</p>

<p>Indeed, both sides send <m>x</m> to</p>

<p>
<md>h(g(f(x))).</md>
</p>

<p>Thus parentheses may be omitted in long compositions when the order is clear.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-identity-composition">
<title>Identity and Composition</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{id}_B\circ f=f</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>f\circ\operatorname{id}_A=f.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus identity functions behave as identity elements for function composition.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-function-composition">
<title>Worked Example: Function Composition</title>
<example>
<title>Compose Two Functions</title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=2x+1,\qquad g(x)=x^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Find <m>g\circ f</m>.</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>(g\circ f)(x)=(2x+1)^2=4x^2+4x+1</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-inverse-functions">
<title>Inverse Functions</title>
<p>The notation</p>

<p>
<md>f^{-1}</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ inverse.</q></p>

<definition>
<title>Inverse Function</title>
<statement>
<p>If <m>f:A\to B</m>, an <em>inverse function</em> is a function</p>

<p>
<md>f^{-1}:B\to A</md>
</p>

<p>such that</p>

<p>
<md>f^{-1}\circ f=\operatorname{id}_A</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>f\circ f^{-1}=\operatorname{id}_B.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The inverse reverses the action of <m>f</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-inverse-not-reciprocal">
<title>Inverse Functions Are Not Reciprocals</title>
<p>The notation</p>

<p>
<md>f^{-1}(x)</md>
</p>

<p>does not mean</p>

<p>
<md>\frac1{f(x)}.</md>
</p>

<p>These are entirely different operations.</p>

<p>For example, if</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=2x+3,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>f^{-1}(x)=\frac{x-3}{2}.</md>
</p>

<p>But</p>

<p>
<md>\frac1{f(x)}=\frac1{2x+3}.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-finding-inverse-algebraically">
<title>Finding an Inverse Algebraically</title>
<p>To find the inverse of a bijective real-valued function given by a formula, a
common procedure is:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>write <m>y=f(x)</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>solve the equation for <m>x</m> in terms of <m>y</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>interchange the variable names;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>verify the compositions if needed.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-finding-inverse">
<title>Worked Example: Finding an Inverse</title>
<example>
<title>Find an Inverse Function</title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R\to\R,\qquad f(x)=4x-7.</md>
</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>f^{-1}(x)=\frac{x+7}{4}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-invertibility-bijectivity">
<title>Invertibility and Bijectivity</title>
<p>A function has a genuine inverse function exactly when it is bijective.</p>

<p>If a function is not injective, two different inputs produce the same output,
so the inverse would not know which input to return.</p>

<p>If a function is not surjective, some elements of the codomain have no
preimage, so the inverse could not assign them outputs.</p>

<p>Thus bijectivity is precisely the condition required for invertibility.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-domain-restriction-inverse">
<title>Restricting a Domain to Obtain an Inverse</title>
<p>The function</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R\to[0,\infty),\qquad f(x)=x^2</md>
</p>

<p>is surjective but not injective.</p>

<p>However, restricting its domain gives</p>

<p>
<md>f:[0,\infty)\to[0,\infty),\qquad f(x)=x^2.</md>
</p>

<p>This restricted function is bijective.</p>

<p>Its inverse is</p>

<p>
<md>f^{-1}(x)=\sqrt x.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-images-of-sets">
<title>Images of Sets</title>
<p>Functions can act on entire subsets of their domains.</p>

<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>S\subseteq A</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>f:A\to B,</md>
</p>

<p>then the image of <m>S</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>f(S)=\{f(x):x\in S\}.</md>
</p>

<p>This is read</p>

<p><q>the image of @@PROTECTED0@@ under @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-image-set">
<title>Worked Example: Image of a Set</title>
<example>
<title>Find the Image of a Set</title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=x^2</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>S=\{-2,-1,0,1,2\}.</md>
</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>f(S)=\{0,1,4\}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-preimages-of-sets">
<title>Preimages of Sets</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>T\subseteq B,</md>
</p>

<p>the preimage of <m>T</m> under <m>f:A\to B</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>f^{-1}(T)=\{x\in A:f(x)\in T\}.</md>
</p>

<p>Here the notation <m>f^{-1}(T)</m> does not require <m>f</m> to have an inverse
function.</p>

<p>It means the set of all inputs whose outputs lie in <m>T</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-preimage-set">
<title>Worked Example: Preimage of a Set</title>
<example>
<title>Find a Preimage</title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R\to\R,\qquad f(x)=x^2</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>T=[1,4].</md>
</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>f^{-1}([1,4])=[-2,-1]\cup[1,2]</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-image-laws">
<title>Image Laws</title>
<p>For subsets</p>

<p>
<md>S,T\subseteq A,</md>
</p>

<p>we always have</p>

<p>
<md>f(S\cup T)=f(S)\cup f(T).</md>
</p>

<p>For intersections,</p>

<p>
<md>f(S\cap T)\subseteq f(S)\cap f(T).</md>
</p>

<p>Equality need not hold unless additional conditions, such as injectivity, are
present.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-preimage-laws">
<title>Preimage Laws</title>
<p>Preimages interact especially well with set operations.</p>

<p>For subsets</p>

<p>
<md>U,V\subseteq B,</md>
</p>

<p>we have</p>

<p>
<md>f^{-1}(U\cup V)=f^{-1}(U)\cup f^{-1}(V),</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>f^{-1}(U\cap V)=f^{-1}(U)\cap f^{-1}(V),</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>f^{-1}(U^c)=\left(f^{-1}(U)\right)^c.</md>
</p>

<p>These identities become important throughout analysis and topology.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-sequences-as-functions">
<title>Sequences as Functions</title>
<p>A sequence can be viewed as a function whose domain is a set of integers,
usually <m>\N</m>.</p>

<p>A sequence may be written</p>

<p>
<md>a:\N\to\R.</md>
</p>

<p>Instead of <m>a(n)</m>, sequence notation usually writes</p>

<p>
<md>a_n.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>a_n=a(n).</md>
</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>a_n=\frac1n</md>
</p>

<p>defines the sequence</p>

<p>
<md>1,\frac12,\frac13,\frac14,\ldots.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-multivariable-functions">
<title>Multivariable Functions</title>
<p>Functions need not take only one input coordinate.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R^2\to\R</md>
</p>

<p>may be defined by</p>

<p>
<md>f(x,y)=x^2+y^2.</md>
</p>

<p>This is read</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ maps R squared to R</q></p>

<p>and</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ of @@PROTECTED1@@ equals @@PROTECTED2@@.</q></p>

<p>The input is an ordered pair</p>

<p>
<md>(x,y)\in\R^2.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-vector-valued-functions">
<title>Vector-Valued Functions</title>
<p>A function may also produce several output coordinates.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>F:\R\to\R^2,\qquad F(t)=(\cos t,\sin t).</md>
</p>

<p>This function assigns each real number <m>t</m> a point in the plane.</p>

<p>Such functions are studied extensively in vector calculus, differential
equations, and geometry.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-functions-general-sets">
<title>Functions Between General Sets</title>
<p>Functions need not involve numbers at all.</p>

<p>For example, suppose <m>P</m> is a set of people and <m>D</m> is a set of calendar
dates.</p>

<p>A function</p>

<p>
<md>b:P\to D</md>
</p>

<p>might assign each person their birth date.</p>

<p>Likewise, a function could map graphs to numbers, matrices to matrices, sets
to sets, or functions to other functions.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-common-function-errors">
<title>Common Function Errors</title>
<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-f-x-with-multiplication">
<title>Confusing <m>f(x)</m> with Multiplication</title>
<p>The expression</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)</md>
</p>

<p>means the value of the function at <m>x</m>.</p>

<p>It does not generally mean <m>f\cdot x</m>.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-function-error-domain">
<title>Ignoring the Domain</title>
<p>A formula alone does not completely determine a function.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\frac1{x-3}</md>
</p>

<p>is undefined at <m>x=3</m>.</p>

<p>Any function using this formula must exclude <m>3</m> from its domain.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-function-error-range-codomain">
<title>Confusing Range and Codomain</title>
<p>The codomain is specified as part of the function.</p>

<p>The range consists only of outputs actually obtained.</p>

<p>They need not be equal.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-function-error-inverse">
<title>Assuming Every Function Has an Inverse</title>
<p>A function must be bijective to possess an inverse function between its full
domain and codomain.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-function-error-inverse-reciprocal">
<title>Confusing Inverse Function and Reciprocal</title>
<p>The expressions</p>

<p>
<md>f^{-1}(x)</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\frac1{f(x)}</md>
</p>

<p>usually mean different things.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-function-error-composition">
<title>Reversing Composition Order</title>
<p>The expression</p>

<p>
<md>(g\circ f)(x)</md>
</p>

<p>means</p>

<p>
<md>g(f(x)).</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore <m>f</m> acts first.</p>
</subsubsection>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-functions-worked-examples">
<title>Worked Examples</title>
<example>
<title>Determine Whether a Relation Is a Function</title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>R=\{(1,a),(2,b),(3,b),(4,c)\}.</md>
</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>Yes. Every input has exactly one output, so <m>R</m> defines a function.</p>
</example>

<example>
<title>Find the Natural Domain</title>
<p>Find the real natural domain of</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=\frac{\sqrt{x-2}}{x-5}.</md>
</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\dom(f)=[2,5)\cup(5,\infty)</md>
</p>
</example>

<example>
<title>Test Injectivity</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R\to\R,\qquad f(x)=x^3+1.</md>
</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p><m>f</m> is injective.</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-functions-exercises">
<title>Exercises</title>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=3x-4.</md>
</p>

<p>Find</p>

<p>
<md>f(2),\qquad f(-1),\qquad f(0).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether</p>

<p>
<md>\{(1,a),(2,b),(3,a)\}</md>
</p>

<p>defines a function.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether</p>

<p>
<md>\{(1,a),(1,b),(2,c)\}</md>
</p>

<p>defines a function.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the natural real domain of</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=\frac1{x+4}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the natural real domain of</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=\sqrt{5-x}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=
\begin{cases}
2x+1,&amp;x\geq0,\\
x^2,&amp;x&lt;0.
\end{cases}</md>
</p>

<p>Find <m>f(3)</m>, <m>f(-2)</m>, and <m>f(0)</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R\to\R,\qquad f(x)=5x+2</md>
</p>

<p>is injective.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R\to\R,\qquad f(x)=x^2</md>
</p>

<p>is surjective.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R\to[0,\infty),\qquad f(x)=x^2</md>
</p>

<p>is surjective.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether</p>

<p>
<md>f:\R\to\R,\qquad f(x)=x^3</md>
</p>

<p>is bijective.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=x+2,\qquad g(x)=3x.</md>
</p>

<p>Find</p>

<p>
<md>(g\circ f)(x)</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>(f\circ g)(x).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the inverse of</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=5x-9.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Verify your inverse from the previous exercise by computing</p>

<p>
<md>f^{-1}(f(x))</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>f(f^{-1}(x)).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=x^2</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>S=\{-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3\}.</md>
</p>

<p>Find <m>f(S)</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=x^2,</md>
</p>

<p>find</p>

<p>
<md>f^{-1}([4,9]).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Give an example of a function that is injective but not surjective.
Clearly specify its domain and codomain.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Give an example of a function that is surjective but not injective.
Clearly specify its domain and codomain.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why the codomain matters when deciding whether a function is
surjective.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that the composition of two injective functions is injective.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that the composition of two surjective functions is surjective.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove that the composition of two bijective functions is bijective.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove</p>

<p>
<md>f^{-1}(U\cup V)=f^{-1}(U)\cup f^{-1}(V).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove</p>

<p>
<md>f^{-1}(U\cap V)=f^{-1}(U)\cap f^{-1}(V).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-functions-connections">
<title>Section Connections</title>
<remark>
<title>Chapter Connection</title>
<p>Functions build directly on relations from <xref ref="sec-relations" /> and become
foundational throughout the rest of mathematics. Calculus studies rates of
change and accumulation of functions; linear algebra studies linear
transformations; probability studies random variables; statistics studies
models and estimators; topology and analysis rely heavily on images and
preimages; and abstract algebra studies functions preserving algebraic
structure.</p>
</remark>
</subsection>

</section>
